<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">ARBED</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1911–2002</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Aciéries Réunies de Burbach-Eich-Dudelange</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">ARBED was a Luxembourg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> company formed in 1911 by the merger of several ironworks and steelworks. Using the minette iron ore of southern Luxembourg, it became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s leading steel producers and the country's largest employer, with plants in Luxembourg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. In 2002 it merged with Usinor and Aceralia to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcelor">Arcelor</a>.</p></div>
